== Exercise 1: predecessor pointers
OK   LeyzorekAPSP, its pair version and FloydWarshall compute the true distances (no negative cycles)  [5000 random graphs, weights -2..3, 1470 with a zero cycle]
OK   1(a) plain pred update in LeyzorekAPSP fails on some graphs, and only on graphs with a zero-length cycle  [154 failures]
OK   1(a) (length, #edges) pairs compared lexicographically: pointers always trace shortest paths
OK   1(b) FloydWarshall pred update (plain, and with pairs): pointers always trace shortest paths, zero cycles included
OK   1(a) counterexample: Leyzorek distances from d are 3, 2, 1 to a, b, c and are correct  [[3, 2, 1, 0]]
OK   1(a) counterexample: pred[d,a] = b and pred[d,b] = a (a pointer cycle), pred[d,c] = d
OK   1(a) counterexample trace: (c,a) via b; (d,a) via c gets pred b; (d,b) via a gets pred a; nothing else  [[(2, 0, 1, 2, 1), (3, 0, 2, 3, 1), (3, 1, 0, 2, 0)]]
OK   1(a) pairs fix the counterexample: (d,b) = (2, 2 edges) with pred c; FloydWarshall gets pred[d,b] = c too
== Exercise 2: correct distances with negative cycles
OK   2(a) Johnson + bad components (Bellman-Ford per strong component) + reachability = brute force  [2500 random graphs, weights -3..3, 985 with a negative cycle]
OK   2(b) LeyzorekAPSP + negative-diagonal marking (in place, and into a copy) = brute force
OK   2(c) FloydWarshall + negative-diagonal marking (in place, and into a copy) = brute force
OK   2 lemma: on a negative cycle => negative diagonal => in a strong component with a negative cycle
== Exercise 3: returning a negative cycle
OK   3(a) Johnson: Bellman-Ford pointer walk returns a simple negative cycle, else the distances  [2500 random graphs, 1137 with a negative cycle]
OK   3(b) Leyzorek: negative diagonal, then Bellman-Ford from x returns a simple negative cycle
OK   3(c) Floyd-Warshall: negative diagonal, then Bellman-Ford from x returns a simple negative cycle
OK   3(c) Floyd-Warshall without Bellman-Ford: the pre-phase test u~>r~>u returns a simple negative cycle
== Exercise 4: zero-length cycles
OK   4(a) a zero cycle exists iff the zero-reduced-weight subgraph Z has a cycle; DFS returns one  [3000 random graphs without negative cycles, 731 with a zero cycle]
OK   4(b) H = edges of Z inside a strong component of Z: all cycles zero, every zero cycle kept, = union of zero cycles
OK   4 figure: prices pi = (-2, 0, -3, -4, -2, -2) for a..f  [[-2, 0, -3, -4, -2, -2]]
OK   4 figure: reduced weights a->b 0, b->c 0, c->a 0, b->d 3, d->e 0, e->d 0, c->f 0, f->e 0, e->b 1  [{(0, 1): 0, (1, 2): 0, (2, 0): 0, (1, 3): 3, (3, 4): 0, (4, 3): 0, (2, 5): 0, (5, 4): 0, (4, 1): 1}]
OK   4 figure: no negative cycle; zero cycles are exactly a->b->c->a and d->e->d  [[[0, 1, 2], [3, 4]]]
OK   4 figure: Z = H + {c->f, f->e}; H = {a->b, b->c, c->a, d->e, e->d}; DFS on Z finds a zero cycle  [Z=[(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 0), (2, 5), (3, 4), (4, 3), (5, 4)] H=[(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 0), (3, 4), (4, 3)]]
OK   4 figure: other cycles b->d->e->b and b->c->f->e->b have lengths 4 and 1
== Exercise 5: distances between k vertex classes
OK   5 reweight once, then one Dijkstra per class from a super-source with edges z->x of weight -pi(x)  [2500 random graphs and partitions]
OK   5 trap: weight-0 super-source edges in the reweighted graph give wrong answers  [wrong on 943 of 2500]
== Exercise 6: arbitrage
OK   6(a) MaxAmt by Bellman-Ford on products = best product over all simple paths from currency 1  [1500 random markets, 601 with arbitrage]
OK   6(a) the -ln weights version gives the same amounts (floating point, rel. error 1e-9)
OK   6(b) Bellman-Ford from an artificial source detects exactly the markets with a cycle of product > 1
OK   6(c) the extracted cycle is a simple cycle with product > 1
OK   6 book example: 1 USD -> 120 JPY -> 1.2 EUR -> 1.44 USD, cycle weight -ln 1.44 < 0  [36/25]
== Exercise 7: Morty
OK   7 min over (s, p, f) of dist(s,p)+dist(p,f)+dist(f,s) = min over (p, f) of dist(p,f)+dist(f,p)  [1500 random graphs with weights 0..9 and random thresholds]
OK   7 the start vertex s = p attains the optimum
== Exercise 8: deleting a vertex
OK   8(a) G' with w'(u->w) = min(w(u->w), w(u->v)+w(v->w)) keeps all distances among V - {v}  [2500 random graphs without negative cycles, weights -3..5]
OK   8(b) dist(v,x) = min over v->y of w(v->y)+dist'(y,x), and symmetrically dist(x,v)
OK   8(c) eliminate-recurse-extend APSP = brute force
OK   8(c) work recurrence T(V) = T(V-1) + O(V^2): T(V)/V^3 -> 1 (exact count (V-1)(V-2) + 2(V-1)^2 per level)  [0.9950]
OK   8 figure: deleting v from u1->v (2), u2->v (-1), v->x1 (3), v->x2 (1), u1->x1 (4), x2->u2 (2)  [{(0, 3): 4, (0, 4): 3, (1, 3): 2, (1, 4): 0, (4, 1): 2}]
